WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

1
00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:05.850
The human body is made up of 78 organs,
206 bones, and trillions of cells. This

2
00:00:05.860 --> 00:00:09.670
enormous number of parts needs to be in
constant contact, despite the huge

3
00:00:09.670 --> 00:00:13.360
distance that separates them. Small
messengers, called hormones, are what

4
00:00:13.360 --> 00:00:17.440
help us communicate a message from one
part of the body to another. Hormones

5
00:00:17.440 --> 00:00:20.620
are involved in many important
biological processes, regulating

6
00:00:20.620 --> 00:00:25.060
reproduction, stress, inflammation and
metabolism. Let's say you just ate a

7
00:00:25.060 --> 00:00:28.320
big meal. As your stomach breaks down
the food, it makes a sugar called

8
00:00:28.330 --> 00:00:32.070
glucose. Glucose will travel from the
stomach to the bloodstream. Eventually,

9
00:00:32.080 --> 00:00:36.630
it makes it to the pancreas, one of the
body's many glands. Glands act like

10
00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:40.700
post offices for the body. These glands
can sense what's happening in the blood,

11
00:00:40.710 --> 00:00:44.520
like all that glucose from your meal.
They then release different hormones as

12
00:00:44.520 --> 00:00:48.670
letters that tell the rest of the body
how to respond. After the glucose makes

13
00:00:48.670 --> 00:00:53.080
it to the pancreas gland, the pancreas
releases a hormone called insulin. This

14
00:00:53.090 --> 00:00:55.770
insulin will go back into the
bloodstream and travel throughout the

15
00:00:55.770 --> 00:00:59.630
whole body, telling your fat cells to
start absorbing more sugar from the

16
00:00:59.630 --> 00:01:03.580
bloodstream to get your glucose levels
back to normal. The post office has to

17
00:01:03.580 --> 00:01:06.940
make sure that the correct hormone
letters get to the correct mailbox, or

18
00:01:06.940 --> 00:01:11.100
cell. For example, insulin tells cells
to absorb sugar from the bloodstream.

19
00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:14.930
But adrenaline, a stress hormone, can
tell the body to get ready to fight.

20
00:01:15.150 --> 00:01:18.760
What happens if something goes wrong
with this delivery process? What if

21
00:01:18.770 --> 00:01:22.810
something happens to the mailbox? For
example, the mailbox might already be

22
00:01:22.810 --> 00:01:26.940
stuffed with letters, so no new insulin
letters can it. That's what happens in

23
00:01:26.940 --> 00:01:31.360
type 2 diabetes. Insulin receptors can
no longer detect insulin, and the body

24
00:01:31.360 --> 00:01:35.270
can't regulate blood sugar anymore. Or
something could go wrong at the post

25
00:01:35.270 --> 00:01:38.970
office. Let's say the letters don't get
properly sorted to be delivered. The

26
00:01:38.970 --> 00:01:42.540
mailbox still works fine, but there are
no letters being sent. This is what

27
00:01:42.540 --> 00:01:46.300
happens in type 1 diabetes. Luckily,
because the mailboxes can still

28
00:01:46.310 --> 00:01:49.600
received insulin, we can get around the
slow down at the post office by

29
00:01:49.600 --> 00:01:52.890
introducing insulin into the
bloodstream. Our body has a variety of

30
00:01:52.890 --> 00:01:56.540
different hormones that tell cells
respond in different ways, but all of

31
00:01:56.540 --> 00:02:00.370
them share the same basic delivery
system. Glands receive a signal to

32
00:02:00.370 --> 00:02:03.360
produce a hormone and then they send
out that hormone to the rest of the

33
00:02:03.360 --> 00:02:07.290
body. It's an effective way to get all
the parts of the body on the same page

34
00:02:07.300 --> 00:02:09.150
to coordinate important functions.

